== Problem ==
Problems 8, 9 and 10 use the data found in the accompanying paragraph and figures

Four friends, Art, Roger, Paul and Trisha, bake cookies, and all cookies have the same thickness. The shapes of the cookies differ, as shown.

<imath>\circ</imath> Art's cookies are trapezoids: 
<asy>size(80);defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));defaultpen(fontsize(8));
draw(origin--(5,0)--(5,3)--(2,3)--cycle);
draw(rightanglemark((5,3), (5,0), origin));
label("5 in", (2.5,0), S);
label("3 in", (5,1.5), E);
label("3 in", (3.5,3), N);</asy>

<imath>\circ</imath> Roger's cookies are rectangles: 
<asy>size(80);defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));defaultpen(fontsize(8));
draw(origin--(4,0)--(4,2)--(0,2)--cycle);
draw(rightanglemark((4,2), (4,0), origin));
draw(rightanglemark((0,2), origin, (4,0)));
label("4 in", (2,0), S);
label("2 in", (4,1), E);</asy>

<imath>\circ</imath> Paul's cookies are parallelograms: 
<asy>size(80);defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));defaultpen(fontsize(8));
draw(origin--(3,0)--(2.5,2)--(-0.5,2)--cycle);
draw((2.5,2)--(2.5,0), dashed);
draw(rightanglemark((2.5,2),(2.5,0), origin));
label("3 in", (1.5,0), S);
label("2 in", (2.5,1), W);</asy>

<imath>\circ</imath> Trisha's cookies are triangles: 
<asy>size(80);defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));defaultpen(fontsize(8));
draw(origin--(3,0)--(3,4)--cycle);
draw(rightanglemark((3,4),(3,0), origin));
label("3 in", (1.5,0), S);
label("4 in", (3,2), E);</asy>

How many cookies will be in one batch of Trisha's cookies?

<imath> \textbf{(A)}\ 10\qquad\textbf{(B)}\ 12\qquad\textbf{(C)}\ 16\qquad\textbf{(D)}\ 18\qquad\textbf{(E)}\ 24</imath>

== Solution 1==
Art's cookies have areas of <imath> 3 \cdot 3 + \frac{2 \cdot 3}{2}=9+3=12 </imath>. There are 12 cookies in one of Art's batches so everyone used <imath> 12 \cdot 12=144 \text{ in}^2 </imath> of dough. Trisha's cookies have an area of <imath> \frac{3 \cdot 4}{2}=6 </imath> so she has <imath> \frac{144}{6}=\boxed{\textbf{(E)}\ 24}</imath> cookies per batch.

==Solution 2==
Using the same method as the previous solution you can find that Art's cookies have a area of 12 and Trisha's cookies have an area of 6. Because they both used the same amount of dough and Trisha's cookies use half the dough compared to Art's you can just multiply the total number of cookies Art makes by 2 to balance out the dough used. This gives <imath>12 \cdot 2</imath> which is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(E)}\ 24}</imath>.

